      },
      "text": "## The Three Appeals"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#012",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:rhetoric:00-overview, git-history:rhetoric:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "rhetoric/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "rhetoric/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:rhetoric:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "rhetoric",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "rhetoric",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Rhetoric - Overview and Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nTHREE APPEALS (PISTEIS / MEANS OF PERSUASION)\n----------------------------------------------\nLOGOS (reason/argument)\n  The logical substance of the argument.\n  Evidence, inference, structure.\n  Deductive: from premises to conclusion (syllogism, enthymeme)\n  Inductive: from examples to general conclusion (paradigm)\n  NOT just about being logical — about being perceived\n  as offering a well-reasoned case."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#013",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:rhetoric:00-overview, git-history:rhetoric:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "rhetoric/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "rhetoric/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:rhetoric:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "rhetoric",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "rhetoric",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Rhetoric - Overview and Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "ETHOS (character/credibility)\n  The speaker's credibility as perceived by the audience.\n  Three components (Aristotle):\n    Phronesis (practical wisdom): you know what you're\n      talking about; you have experience and judgment\n    Arete (virtue): you are honest; you don't have\n      a hidden agenda; you're acting in good faith\n    Eunoia (goodwill): you care about the audience's\n      interests, not just your own"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#014",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:rhetoric:00-overview, git-history:rhetoric:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "rhetoric/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "rhetoric/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:rhetoric:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "rhetoric",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "rhetoric",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Rhetoric - Overview and Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "CRITICAL POINT: Ethos is constructed IN the speech,\n  not brought to it from outside.\n  Your reputation helps, but what you say and how you\n  say it in this speech is what matters for ethos."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#015",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:rhetoric:00-overview, git-history:rhetoric:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "rhetoric/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "rhetoric/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:rhetoric:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "rhetoric",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "rhetoric",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Rhetoric - Overview and Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "PATHOS (emotion/audience engagement)\n  The emotional state of the audience.\n  Aristotle: you must put the audience in a specific\n  emotional state to receive your argument.\n  Fear, hope, anger, compassion, indignation — each\n  disposes an audience differently.\n  NOT manipulation in a pejorative sense — APPROPRIATE\n  emotional engagement is part of rational persuasion.\n  A jury that feels nothing will not convict; they must\n  feel the appropriate indignation, sympathy, etc."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#016",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:rhetoric:00-overview, git-history:rhetoric:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "rhetoric/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "rhetoric/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:rhetoric:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "rhetoric",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "rhetoric",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Rhetoric - Overview and Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "THE INTERPLAY:\n  Effective rhetoric integrates all three.\n  A logos-only speech is cold and may be dismissed.\n  A pathos-only speech is emotional manipulation.\n  An ethos-only speech depends entirely on reputation.\n  The classic Aristotelian speech: established ethos\n  (you should trust my judgment because...) + logos\n  (here is the argument) + pathos (here is why this\n  matters to you personally).\n```"
    },
    {
